        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "example_question",
            "normalized_html": "<p>How many solutions does the equation below have?\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 27x = -3x]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "How many solutions does the equation below have?\n[MATH: 27x = -3x]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
            "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We are given the equation\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 27x = -3x.]</span></p><p>First, we apply the addition principle to isolate the variable terms. Adding <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3x]</span> to both sides, we get\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 27x + 3x|= \u2212 3x + 3x; 30x|= 0.]</span></p><p>Now, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 30\\mathbin{:}]</span>\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: x = 0]</span></p><p>Since we found a single unique value of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span> that satisfies the equation, the equation has exactly one solution.</p><p>Therefore, the correct answer is that the equation has <strong>exactly one</strong> solution.</p>",
            "readable_text": "We are given the equation\n[MATH: 27x = -3x.]\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle to isolate the variable terms. Adding [MATH: 3x] to both sides, we get\n[MATH: 27x + 3x|= \u2212 3x + 3x; 30x|= 0.]\n\nNow, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by [MATH: 30\\mathbin{:}]\n[MATH: x = 0]\n\nSince we found a single unique value of [MATH: x] that satisfies the equation, the equation has exactly one solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is that the equation has exactly one solution.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Example Prompt:\nHow many solutions does the equation below have?\n[MATH: 27x = -3x]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe are given the equation\n[MATH: 27x = -3x.]\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle to isolate the variable terms. Adding [MATH: 3x] to both sides, we get\n[MATH: 27x + 3x|= \u2212 3x + 3x; 30x|= 0.]\n\nNow, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by [MATH: 30\\mathbin{:}]\n[MATH: x = 0]\n\nSince we found a single unique value of [MATH: x] that satisfies the equation, the equation has exactly one solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is that the equation has exactly one solution."
      },
