    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nIn the math section of the SAT exam, you may be asked to identify conditions where a linear equation has zero, one, or infinitely many solutions. This concept often appears in questions such as: \u201cIf this equation has no solution, what must be true about the constants?\u201d\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Determining the Number of Solutions to a One-Step Equation\n\nExample Prompt:\nHow many solutions does the equation below have?\n[MATH: 27x = -3x]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe are given the equation\n[MATH: 27x = -3x.]\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle to isolate the variable terms. Adding [MATH: 3x] to both sides, we get\n[MATH: 27x + 3x|= \u2212 3x + 3x; 30x|= 0.]\n\nNow, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by [MATH: 30\\mathbin{:}]\n[MATH: x = 0]\n\nSince we found a single unique value of [MATH: x] that satisfies the equation, the equation has exactly one solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is that the equation has exactly one solution.\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nHow many solutions does the equation below have?\n[MATH: \u2212 36x = \u2212 12x]\n\nChoices:\na. Exactly two\nb. Zero\nc. Impossible to determine\nd. Exactly one\ne. Infinitely many\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nConsider the equation\n\n[MATH: 48x = 48x.]\n\nHow many solutions does this equation have?\n\nChoices:\na. Impossible to determine\nb. Exactly one\nc. Infinitely many\nd. Zero\ne. Exactly two\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Determining the Number of Solutions to a Linear Equation Containing Binomials\n\nExample Prompt:\nHow many solutions does the equation [MATH: -6(x - 10) = 2(30 - x)] have?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe start by expanding the products on both sides of the equation and simplifying:\n\n[MATH: \u2212 6(x \u2212 10)|= 2(30 \u2212 x); \u2212 6x + 60|= 60 \u2212 2x; \u2212 4x|= 0; x|= 0]\n\nTherefore, this equation has exactly one solution, [MATH: x = 0.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3x \u2212 7 = 2x + 1]\nHow many solutions exist to the equation shown above?\n\nChoices:\na. Exactly [MATH: 3]\nb. Exactly [MATH: 1]\nc. Exactly [MATH: 2]\nd. Infinitely many\ne. None\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nHow many solutions does the equation [MATH: 5(3x + 7) = 4(3x + 7)] have?\n\nChoices:\na. Exactly one\nb. Exactly two\nc. Zero\nd. Impossible to determine\ne. Infinitely many\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Other Cases\n\nExample Prompt:\nHow many solutions does the equation [MATH: 3(x + 2) = 7(x - 1) - 4x] have?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe begin by expanding the products in our equation, and then collecting like terms:\n\n[MATH: 3(x + 2)|= 7(x \u2212 1) \u2212 4x; 3x + 6|= 7x \u2212 7 \u2212 4x; 3x \u2212 7x + 4x|= \u2212 7 \u2212 6; 0|= \u2212 13]\n\nSince this equality is false for any value of [MATH: x,] the equation has no solutions.\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 7 \u2212 9x = \u2212 (1)/(12)(108x \u2212 84)]\n\nHow many solutions does the equation above have?\n\nChoices:\na. Zero\nb. Exactly one\nc. More than three\nd. Exactly three\ne. Exactly two\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nHow many solutions does the equation [MATH: 8(6x + 2) = 4(12x + 4)] have?\n\nChoices:\na. Infinitely many\nb. Exactly one\nc. Impossible to determine\nd. Zero\ne. Exactly two"
